Multilevel Inheritance – Person, Employee and Manager

📌 Description

This C++ program demonstrates Multilevel Inheritance using three classes:

- "Person" → Base class
- "Employee" → Derived from "Person"
- "Manager" → Derived from "Employee"

The "Manager" class can access the features inherited through both "Employee" and "Person".

---

📊 Class Hierarchy

Person
   ↓
Employee
   ↓
Manager

---

📚 Classes Used

1. "Person" Class

The "Person" class is the base class.

It contains:

- "name" – Stores the person's name.
- Constructor – Initializes the person's name.
- "showPerson()" – Displays the person's name.
- "name" is declared as "protected", allowing derived classes to access it.

---

2. "Employee" Class

The "Employee" class inherits from "Person".

class Employee : public Person

It contains:

- "employeeId" – Stores the employee ID.
- "showEmployee()" – Displays the employee ID.
- Constructor – Calls the "Person" constructor to initialize the name.

---

3. "Manager" Class

The "Manager" class inherits from "Employee".

class Manager : public Employee

It contains:

- "teamSize" – Stores the number of team members.
- "showManager()" – Displays the manager's complete details.

Inside "showManager()":

- "showPerson()" displays the name.
- "showEmployee()" displays the employee ID.
- "teamSize" displays the team size.

---

⚙️ Main Function

The following "Manager" object is created:

Manager manager("Ravi", 501, 8);

Where:

- ""Ravi"" → Manager's name
- "501" → Employee ID
- "8" → Team size

Then:

manager.showManager();

is used to display all the details.

---

🖥️ Output

Name: Ravi
Employee ID: 501
Team Size: 8

---

🧠 OOP Concept Used

Multilevel Inheritance

Person
  ↓
Employee
  ↓
Manager

Here:

- "Person" → Base class
- "Employee" → Derived from "Person"
- "Manager" → Derived from "Employee"
- Inheritance Type → Multilevel Inheritance

"Employee" inherits from "Person", and "Manager" inherits from "Employee". This forms a chain of inheritance, which is known as Multilevel Inheritance.

